    "result_text": "All 22 tests pass (BUILD SUCCESS), including the new Cp13 namesake-count test.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `namesakeCount` field on created owners \u2014 the number of existing owners (before this create) sharing the same firstName and lastName, compared case-insensitively.\n\nFollowing the existing pattern used for `customerCode` and `householdId` (a create-time value persisted so `GET /api/owners/{id}` returns it later), I made these changes:\n\n- **`AssignNamesakeCount.java`** (new function): runs in the create-owner pipeline after `BuildOwner` and before `SaveOwner`. It counts existing owners whose firstName and lastName both match (case-insensitive) and stamps the count onto the built owner in place. Because it runs before the save, the owner being created is excluded \u2014 so the first owner of a name gets 0.\n- **`owners.POST.yml`**: wired a new `namesakeCount` step into the pipeline between `customerCode` and `save`, under the same `transaction` governance.\n- **`Owner.java`**: added the persisted `namesakeCount` (`Integer`, column `namesake_count`) with getter/setter.\n- **Schema SQL** (hsqldb, h2, postgres, mysql): added the `namesake_count` column to the `owners` table.\n- **`openapi.yml`**: added `namesakeCount` as a read-only `int32` (minimum 0) on the `Owner` schema, so it's generated into `OwnerDto` and returned in responses.\n- **`OwnerMapper.java`**: ignore `namesakeCount` when building an `Owner` from `OwnerFieldsDto` (it's a server-assigned read-only field, not a request input); the DTO mapping picks it up automatically for responses.\n\nThe full acceptance suite (22 tests) is green.",
